  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Today and Thursday will be the warmest days before the change\n   back to fall-like weather. \n\n-  Choppy lake conditions on lakes for Thursday due to westerly \n   winds between 15 and 25 mph, and locally higher speeds expected\n   over the Divide.\n\n-  Stiff northeast winds are possible Friday night through\n   Saturday creating choppy conditions on area lakes.\n\n-  Cutoff Low brings widespread rain, with snow above 6000 feet \n   Saturday night into Sunday. \n\n\nNorthwesterly flow aloft behind a departing trough of low pressure\nwill keep near-normal daytime high temperatures in the forecast\nfor this afternoon. Expect clear skies across the Northern Rockies\ntoday and tonight. Upper level flow flattens out, becoming more\nwesterly on Thursday as a broad trough of low pressure moves along\nthe Canadian border. There will be enough moisture involved to\nkeep some chances for precipitation in the forecast for northwest\nMontana in the morning. Elsewhere, Thursday looks warm and dry\nwith increased westerly winds. Winds will be gusty enough to\ncreate choppy conditions on Flathead lake and elevated fire\nweather concerns for southwest Montana.  \n\nBy Thursday night, a weak front will become the impetus for\nshowers that continue throughout the day Friday. Northeast winds \ndevelop by Friday evening which will enhance shower activity over\nthe Glacier Region, and also could cause Lake MacDonald to become\nchoppy. \n\nAn impressive pressure gradient sets up on Saturday in response \nto the incoming low pressure system moving onshore over Oregon. \nThere is a 50% chance that sustained winds could exceed 15 mph and\ngusts exceed 25 mph over Flathead Lake. Other areas, especially \nchanneled locations could also experience elevated winds.\n\nA cut-off low pressure system will track from southwest Idaho\nSaturday night to east of Great Falls by Sunday evening, bringing\nwidespread light to moderate precipitation to central Idaho and \nwestern Montana. The heaviest precipitation will be roughly 100 \nmiles north and south from a line from Grangeville, Idaho to \nMissoula to Great Falls with a range from 0.60\" to 1.60\" of total \nprecipitation. The mountains within this line could receive 1 to 2\ninches. This upper trough is a bit colder than the recent system,\nso snow levels could fall to around 6000 feet by daybreak Sunday \nmorning. It could feel very wintry in the backcountry, especially\nalong the Continental Divide and in Glacier National Park.  A \nmoderating and drying trend is expected by early next week.",
